  {# --- the deployables ---------------------------------------------------- #}
  <section aria-labelledby="deploy-heading" class="mt-10">
    <h2 id="deploy-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">The three deployables</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
      Two of them are built from the same repository by two Eleventy configurations that share one
      set of includes and one stylesheet, so the chrome cannot drift between them.
    </p>
    <div class="card">
      <div class="doc-row">
        <span class="doc-method">Site</span>
        <span class="text-sm text-brand-text">
          <strong>tzortzoglou.eu</strong> — the public portfolio. Eleventy, output to
          <code>_site/</code>, served by GitHub Pages. Loads no Firebase at all.
        </span>
        <span class="doc-role">static</span>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-row">
        <span class="doc-method">App</span>
        <span class="text-sm text-brand-text">
          <strong>app.tzortzoglou.eu</strong> — everything behind a sign-in. A second Eleventy
          build of the same repository, output to <code>_app/</code>.
        </span>
        <span class="doc-role">static</span>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-row">
        <span class="doc-method">API</span>
        <span class="text-sm text-brand-text">
          <strong>api.tzortzoglou.eu</strong> — Express, in its own private repository. Holds every
          key and is the only thing that talks to a database.
        </span>
        <span class="doc-role">node</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {# --- where data lives ----------------------------------------------------
     The section this page mostly exists for. "The database" sounds like one thing and is four,
     and three of the four are not the one people mean. #}
  <section aria-labelledby="data-heading" class="mt-10">
    <h2 id="data-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">Where data lives</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
      Four stores, and they hold very different things. The distinction that matters most:
      <strong class="text-brand-text">the weather, the departures and the Formula 1 data are not
      stored anywhere.</strong>
    </p>

    <ul class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">Firebase Authentication</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-1">
          Email addresses and password hashes. No password ever reaches the API — the browser
          exchanges one for a signed token, and the API only ever verifies tokens.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">Firestore</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-1">
          One document per account: name, role, approval state, home location, home stop. The role
          is read from here on <em>every single request</em> rather than trusted from the token.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">Postgres</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-1">
          Three tables and nothing else — contact submissions, a row per process start, and which
          migrations have run. Browsable on
          <a href="/admin/data/" class="text-brand-accent underline hover:no-underline">the data view</a>
          if you are an admin.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">Memory, briefly</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-1">
          Every upstream answer is cached in the API process for between 45 seconds and six hours,
          and lost on restart. Nothing about a bus or a race is ever written to disk, so there is
          no history of either to browse.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  {# --- the tables ---------------------------------------------------------- #}
  <section aria-labelledby="erd-heading" class="mt-10">
    <h2 id="erd-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">The Postgres tables</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
      There are no foreign keys, because nothing references anything. Three independent tables
      written by three unrelated parts of the API — which is worth saying out loud, since an
      entity-relationship diagram normally implies relationships.
    </p>

    <div class="card overflow-x-auto">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 560 208" class="w-full min-w-[520px]" role="img"
        aria-label="Three independent tables. contact_submissions has id, name, email, message, consent_at, notified_at, read_at and created_at, plus ip and user_agent which are withheld from the browser. boots has id, started_at and version. schema_migrations has filename and applied_at.">
        <g>
          <rect x="6" y="8" width="244" height="192" rx="8" class="dg-box"/>
          <rect x="6" y="8" width="244" height="26" rx="8" class="dg-box-accent"/>
          <text x="18" y="26" class="dg-label">contact_submissions</text>
          <text x="18" y="50" class="dg-sub">id · bigserial · PK</text>
          <text x="18" y="66" class="dg-sub">name · text</text>
          <text x="18" y="82" class="dg-sub">email · text</text>
          <text x="18" y="98" class="dg-sub">message · text</text>
          <text x="18" y="114" class="dg-sub">consent_at · timestamptz</text>
          <text x="18" y="130" class="dg-sub">notified_at · timestamptz</text>
          <text x="18" y="146" class="dg-sub">read_at · timestamptz</text>
          <text x="18" y="162" class="dg-sub">created_at · timestamptz</text>
          <text x="18" y="182" class="dg-sub">ip, user_agent — withheld</text>

          <rect x="300" y="8" width="254" height="86" rx="8" class="dg-box"/>
          <rect x="300" y="8" width="254" height="26" rx="8" class="dg-box-accent"/>
          <text x="312" y="26" class="dg-label">boots</text>
          <text x="312" y="50" class="dg-sub">id · bigserial · PK</text>
          <text x="312" y="66" class="dg-sub">started_at · timestamptz</text>
          <text x="312" y="82" class="dg-sub">version · text · nullable</text>

          <rect x="300" y="114" width="254" height="70" rx="8" class="dg-box"/>
          <rect x="300" y="114" width="254" height="26" rx="8" class="dg-box-accent"/>
          <text x="312" y="132" class="dg-label">schema_migrations</text>
          <text x="312" y="156" class="dg-sub">filename · text · PK</text>
          <text x="312" y="172" class="dg-sub">applied_at · timestamptz</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
    </div>

    <p class="hint mt-3 max-w-prose">
      <strong class="text-brand-text">ip</strong> and <strong class="text-brand-text">user_agent</strong>
      exist in the table and are never sent to a browser by any endpoint, including the data view.
      Reading a message does not require knowing where the person was sitting.
    </p>
  </section>

  {# --- endpoints ----------------------------------------------------------- #}
  <section aria-labelledby="api-heading" class="mt-10">
    <h2 id="api-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">The endpoints</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
      Every route the API serves, and the lowest role that may call it. Each one re-reads your role
      and approval from Firestore before doing anything — a token that was valid when it was issued
      is not a claim about what you are allowed to do now.
    </p>

    {# Fetched rather than built into this page, and this is the reason:
       app.tzortzoglou.eu is a directory of static files. Its sign-in is a redirect in
       JavaScript, which decides what is drawn and guards nothing at all - every page here is
       served in full to anyone who asks for the URL. A complete list of endpoints and the role
       each one needs is the most useful thing on this page to somebody who has no account, so
       it is the one part that is not in the HTML. It comes from the API, which asks who you are.

       It also stopped being a hand-kept copy in the process. The list now lives beside the
       router in the API repository, and that repository's test suite fails if the two disagree
       in either direction. #}
    <div id="endpoints" class="mt-4"></div>
    <p id="endpointsNote" role="status" aria-live="polite" class="hint mt-4">Loading the endpoint list…</p>

  </section>

  {# --- upstreams ------------------------------------------------------------ #}
  <section aria-labelledby="up-heading" class="mt-10">
    <h2 id="up-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">The four upstreams</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
      All four are proxied. Two need a credential, which is the hard reason; the other two are
      proxied so one host is called once for everybody rather than once per open tab.
    </p>
    <div class="card">
      <div class="doc-row">
        <span class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-text">Open-Meteo</span>
        <span class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Forecast and air quality. No key. Cached per coordinate.</span>
        <span class="doc-role">no key</span>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-row">
        <span class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-text">Trafiklab ResRobot</span>
        <span class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Swedish departures, arrivals and stop search. Cached 45 seconds — a board older than that is a board that is wrong.</span>
        <span class="doc-role">key</span>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-row">
        <span class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-text">Jolpica</span>
        <span class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Formula 1 calendar, standings and results; the maintained successor to Ergast. Calendar cached six hours, results five minutes.</span>
        <span class="doc-role">no key</span>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-row">
        <span class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-text">GitHub Actions</span>
        <span class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Workflow runs for the Health page. The only source that knows about a deploy that <em>failed</em>.</span>
        <span class="doc-role">key</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="hint mt-3 max-w-prose">
      The two credentials are read from the environment on the server and appear in no response, no
      error message and no log line. Upstream errors are reported by status code only, because an
      error body can echo the request back — and the request carries the key.
    </p>
  </section>

  {# --- conventions ---------------------------------------------------------- #}
  <section aria-labelledby="rules-heading" class="mt-10">
    <h2 id="rules-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">Rules the code follows</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
      Each of these is here because breaking it caused a real bug in this codebase, not because it
      reads well.
    </p>
    <ul class="card space-y-3 text-sm text-brand-text">
      <li>
        <strong>Check the type before coercing a number.</strong> <code>Number(null)</code> is
        <code>0</code>, <code>Number([])</code> is <code>0</code>, and a query string can produce an
        array without being asked. Every parser guards on <code>typeof</code> first.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>An unknown role is the lowest role.</strong> Role names are an explicit list, not
        case-folding. Anything unrecognised resolves to the most restrictive answer, never the
        most permissive.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A comment may not claim a control the code does not have.</strong> If the code
        cannot be made true, the comment changes in the same commit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A signal that was never about the thing being checked proves nothing about it.</strong>
        A green test suite that never loads a module says nothing about that module.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Never invent data on a dashboard.</strong> A panel with nothing behind it says it
        has nothing behind it.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

</section>
